  "verbalization": "Nadolol exhibits a complex network of molecular relationships that suggest a potential interaction with Adrb3, the beta-2 adrenergic receptor. The evidence is grounded in the fact that Nadolol resembles Bupranolol and Clenbuterol, both of which bind to Adrb3. This structural similarity implies that Nadolol may share a similar mode of action, potentially inhibiting Adrb3 through a mechanism involving receptor binding. The lowest common ancestors of Nadolol and its analogs—such as Bupranolol and Clenbuterol—point to a shared class of compounds, namely beta-adrenergic antagonists, which are known to modulate adrenergic signaling. The final scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, supported by the recurring theme of receptor binding and structural similarity. While the evidence is not definitive, the integration of these pathways suggests that Nadolol may act as a beta-adrenergic antagonist, possibly targeting Adrb3, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm this mechanism.",
